WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.410
Writer/director Todd Phillips' gritty origin
story ​Joker,​ starring Joaquin Phoenix

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.210
in the title role, emerged in late 2019 as
one of the biggest surprise hits of the decade

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:14.620
— and all the hub-bub got us wondering…
what would ​Joker 2​ look like?

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.460
All we can do at this point is speculate — but,
you know what?

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.550
We're pretty good at that.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.119
Spoilers for Joker ahead!

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:25.910
Speaking with IndieWire in November 2019,
Todd Phillips set the record straight in regard

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:30.960
to a potential Joker follow-up: He admitted
that the idea of a sequel had certainly been

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.270
discussed, but that there's not a deal in
place yet — and Phoenix hasn't been approached

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.140
about returning at this time.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.140
Phillips dished:

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.070
"Here's the real truth about a sequel.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.440
While Joaquin and I have talked about it,
and while touring the world with Warner Bros

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:49.160
executives... of course, we're sitting at
dinner and they're saying, 'So, have you thought

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.160
about...?'

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.290
But, talking about contracts, there's not
a contract for us to even write a sequel,

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.330
we've never approached Joaquin to be in a
sequel."

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.170
Sorry, folks — that's the cold, hard truth.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.320
"Why are you saying this?

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.369
I don't need you to tell me lies!"

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:08.179
The director also clarified that a purported
meeting with Warner Bros.

00:01:08.179 --> 00:01:13.200
Pictures Group chairman Toby Emmerich — which
was originally misreported by THR — didn't

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.729
go down the way the outlet had described it.

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:20.670
While he copped to pitching a slate of films
featuring DC villains during his initial Joker

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.590
pitch, the notion had swiftly been shot down.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:28.590
It's worth noting that, even in the wake of
THR's jumping the gun, neither Phillips nor

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.850
Phoenix has shot down the idea of Joker 2.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.670
In fact, Phillips might have poured a bit
more gasoline on the flames when asked about

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.929
the idea at an industry event hosted by Deadline
in late 2019.

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:41.049
Phillips said with a chuckle:

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:46.999
"When a movie does $1 billion and cost $60
million to make, of course [the notion of

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:48.280
a sequel] comes up.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.819
But Joaquin and I haven't really decided on
it.

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:51.819
We're open.

00:01:51.819 --> 00:01:54.950
I mean, I'd love to work with him on anything,
quite frankly.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.350
So who knows?"

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:01.219
Phillips implied that if he and Phoenix do
decide to revisit Arthur Fleck's world, though,

00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:05.159
it won't be the potential for more big box
office bucks driving their decision.

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:06.159
He explained:

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:11.180
"[A sequel] would have to have a real thematic
resonance the way this one did, ultimately

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.970
being about childhood trauma and the lack
of love, and the loss of empathy.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:20.920
All those things are really what made this
movie work for us, so we'd have to have something

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.730
that had an equal thematic resonance."

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.879
"Everybody is awful, these days.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:29.060
It's enough to make anyone crazy."

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.430
Even if Joker 2 were to be confirmed tomorrow,
at this early date, we wouldn't have any idea

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:37.420
who might be filling out the flick's cast
— but we do feel pretty safe in stating

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.319
that there's absolutely no way Phoenix wouldn't
return to further plumb the depths of Fleck's

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:44.870
tortured mind if it were to happen.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.250
Phillips has repeatedly made it clear that
there's no way he'd even entertain the possibility

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:55.010
of a sequel if Phoenix, for whom he and Scott
Silver wrote the first movie, weren't on board.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.519
Incidentally, while we can in all likelihood
consider Phoenix a lock if Joker 2 gets the

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:04.750
greenlight, the only other bit of info around
potential casting, which we could confidently

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:11.049
go on record reporting, concerns who we know
wouldn't be in the movie: Robert Pattinson.

00:03:11.049 --> 00:03:15.110
The talented young actor will be portraying
the title character in director Matt Reeves'

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:20.049
upcoming picture The Batman, but Phillips
has strongly implied that Pattinson's Bruce

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:24.239
Wayne and Phoenix's Arthur Fleck exist in
completely different continuities.

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:29.390
Asked at the Toronto International Film Festival
if it were possible that Pattinson's Dark

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:35.189
Knight could appear in a Joker sequel, Phillips
was unequivocal, saying simply, "No, definitely

00:03:35.189 --> 00:03:36.189
not."

00:03:36.189 --> 00:03:40.860
This actually makes a ton of sense — for
as Joker's fans know, the film's relationship

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.000
to DC lore is a complicated one.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.409
Again, any attempt to parse out the plot of
a potential Joker sequel would be an exercise

00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:51.469
in pure guesswork at this point.

00:03:51.469 --> 00:03:55.640
Of course, we can offer at least a couple
guesses of the educated variety — but they

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:59.140
mostly have to do with what the movie wouldn't
be about.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:04.249
First and foremost, as revealed by Phillips'
comments at TIFF, the film would not in any

00:04:04.249 --> 00:04:06.590
way, shape, or form be about Batman.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.890
Yes, Bruce Wayne did appear in Joker — as
a child of perhaps ten, and since Phillips

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:16.780
has been clear on the fact that any sequel
would simply continue to tell Arthur's story,

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.310
it's likely that young Master Bruce would
still be in middle school during the events

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.959
of a potential follow-up.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:26.830
We can infer from Phillips' determination
to keep the focus squarely on Arthur that

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:31.979
any sequel is also unlikely to focus on any
other of Gotham's budding supervillains.

00:04:31.979 --> 00:04:37.889
There just might, however, be a slight chance
that a Joker sequel could introduce a cunning,

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:43.169
cackling threat to Gotham other than Arthur:
the real Joker.

00:04:43.169 --> 00:04:47.550
Follow us here: even since before Joker was
released, fans have speculated that Arthur

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:53.270
may not actually be the Joker with whom we're
all familiar, but rather a sort of proto-Joker

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.500
who serves as a formative influence on the
real deal.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:01.050
The theory is compelling on its own merits,
but it was lent a hell of a lot more credence

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.970
than had been assigned to it previously when
Phillips referred to it in the wake of Joker's

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.889
staggering success.

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:11.460
Phillips raised the possibility unprompted
in a wide-ranging Los Angeles Times interview.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.460
The director said:

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.680
"There's a lot of ways you could look at this
movie.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.740
You could look at it and go, 'This is just
one of his multiple-choice stories.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.090
None of it happened'...

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:25.169
A lot of people I've shown it to have said,
'Oh, I get it — he's just made up a story.

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:26.810
The whole movie is the joke.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.610
It's this thing this guy in Arkham Asylum
concocted.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.259
He might not even be the Joker'...

00:05:32.259 --> 00:05:34.880
Maybe Joaquin's character inspired the Joker.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.979
You don't really know."

00:05:37.979 --> 00:05:40.590
[HAHAHAHAHAHAH]

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:47.979
No, we don't — but if they deign to give
us Joker 2, Phillips, Silver, and Phoenix

00:05:47.979 --> 00:05:50.139
have an opportunity to clear it up for us.

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:53.849
Could we perhaps see the emergence of the
actual Joker in a sequel?

00:05:53.849 --> 00:05:57.650
It's an intriguing possibility, but only time
will tell.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:02.380
Obviously, we don't yet have a release date
for Joker 2, as the involved parties have

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.330
yet to even commence talks.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:08.680
We don't even have an idea as to when the
flick could potentially begin production,

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.370
but it's pretty safe to say that Phillips
and Silver would be inclined to take their

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:17.240
time on the screenplay for a Joker sequel,
crafting the most compelling story possible.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:21.629
In fact, the writing process would likely
be among the lengthiest parts of any sequel's

00:06:21.629 --> 00:06:22.900
road to the screen.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:28.449
We say this because, considering Joker's critical
acclaim and box office clout, everybody involved

00:06:28.449 --> 00:06:33.410
has a strong incentive to make sure that if
the flick does get a follow-up, it's a worthy

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:34.410
one.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:39.080
Rushing a second chapter to the screen would
be folly; the first movie's titanic fan base

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:43.500
will still be there in two or three years,
and even at that time, the indelible mark

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:46.910
left on pop culture by Joker isn't likely
to have faded.

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:50.470
Of course, we'll be keeping our ears to the
ground for any official announcement regarding

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:55.979
Joker 2 — and the minute we have it, we'll
be beating down your door with the news.

